(* tuvm#94 stage 1 gate node — the witness that nsl, not boot-time C, now drives
   the bell.

   Gate 1: a striking at start-up, ordered by nsl. Five bells, because an odd
   count exercises BOTH halves of the ship's-bell phrasing: two pairs and then a
   lone final stroke. Hearing ding-ding … ding-ding … ding is the proof; a single
   stroke would sound identical whether the phrasing survived the move into the
   prim or was lost in it.

   Gate 2: BUTTON_A repeats the LAST striking. That is the gate that actually
   proves the seam is re-entrant and that the amp mute/un-mute is per strike
   rather than a one-shot done at boot — the failure mode this replaces is a bell
   that rings once after reset and is silent forever after.

   Gate 3: a count outside 1-8 raises with its tag rather than striking something
   the convention has no reading for. Same shape as MagTagDisplayNode's
   out-of-grid gate, and worth having ON THE BOARD as well as on the host: the
   check lives in nsl (Bell>>strike:), so this confirms the kernel that shipped in
   the image is the one that carries it.

   `bell strike:` BLOCKS for the whole striking — about half a second a bell, so
   ~2.5 s here. That is safe for the same reason the display's refresh is: the
   pulse mailbox sheds and coalesces rather than aborting (pulse_zephyr.c,
   tuvm#34), so a button pressed mid-strike arrives once afterwards instead of
   being lost. Lean on the button during a striking and you should hear exactly
   one repeat, not a queue of them. *)
class MagTagBellNode packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bell gpio press last |
    space:: platform space.
    bell:: platform bell.
    gpio:: platform gpio.
    last:: 5.

    ('striking ' , last printString) out.
    bell strike: last.
    'struck' out.

    [ bell strike: 9 ]
      on: [ :e | e tag = #bellBounds ]
      do: [ :e | 'gate3 caught bellBounds' out ].

    (* `whenever:do:` is the ONLY subscription selector in this dialect
       (Channel>>whenever:do:) — Space has no `on:do:`, and because nsl dispatches
       dynamically the wrong selector compiles clean and kills the node at run
       time. MagTagDisplayNode died exactly there on its first bench run. *)
    press:: space channel: 'btn/press'.
    press whenever: '' do: [ :m |
      ('repeat ' , last printString) out.
      bell strike: last.
      'struck' out ].
    gpio pin: 15 edge: #falling post: 'A' on: press.

    'ready' out.
    (* `platform pumpEvents` only pumps the backends INTO the space queue;
       `space drain` is what dequeues and fires reactions. A reactor loop without
       the drain enqueues button presses forever and runs none of them. *)
    [ true ] whileTrue: [ platform pumpEvents. space drain ].
  )
) : (
)
